  <graph edgedefault="directed" id="G"><node id="m_adrenergic-vessel"><data key="d0">adrenergic-vessel</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">4</data><data key="d3">receptor to oxidase measured under noise with perturbation; receptor to contraction inferred</data><data key="d4">the sympathetic module makes catecholamines and the vessel module endothelin-1, and neither acts on anything: the constriction half of the two-sided output is missing. Propranolol or phenoxybenzamine each restore relaxation and lower the oxidases and the adhesion molecules under noise, so the receptors are tested; the contraction machinery itself (Gq, PLC, calcium, MLCK, RhoA-ROCK on MYPT1) has never been measured under noise and is drawn as physiology.</data><data key="d5">PMID 39857393, PMID 34616031, PMID 24951589, PMID 28329261</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="1560.0" y="375.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="70.0" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="256.5" x="-8.25" xml:space="preserve" y="10.0" hasText="true">Adrenergic receptors in the
wall: alpha-1 on the smooth
muscle cell, beta-2 on the
endothelium, to myosin<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_aldh2-nitrate"><data key="d0">aldh2-nitrate</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">6</data><data key="d3">readout measured under noise; the enzyme step inferred</data><data key="d4">noise impairs relaxation to nitroglycerine, which is downstream of the endothelium, so the lesion is not only in the synthase; the cyclase block the map already draws is the place it joins. The enzyme's own inhibition is inferred from the functional readout.</data><data key="d5">PMID 32326776</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="0.0" y="450.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="53.5" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="266.0" x="-13.0" xml:space="preserve" y="18.25" hasText="true">The endothelium-independent
limb: aldehyde dehydrogenase
and nitrate bioactivation<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_ampk-nos3"><data key="d0">ampk-nos3</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">1</data><data key="d3">measured under noise, with perturbation</data><data key="d4">the vessel module draws the activating Ser1177 phosphorylation with no kinase, because the noise papers measure the phosphorylation and name no kinase. This one is named and tested: alpha-1 AMPK activation (exercise, intermittent fasting, AICAR) prevents noise-induced dysfunction, and endothelium-specific alpha-1 AMPK deletion abrogates the protection and worsens the injury.</data><data key="d5">PMID 37185661, PMID 28329261</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="0.0" y="600.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="37.0" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="237.5" x="1.25" xml:space="preserve" y="26.5" hasText="true">AMPK on the synthase: the
kinase behind Ser1177<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_cardiac-metabolism"><data key="d0">cardiac-metabolism</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">5</data><data key="d3">measured under noise, with perturbation</data><data key="d4">nothing in the set covers cardiac metabolism, and this is the heart's own phenotype under noise within four days: CPT1B and beta-oxidation, the citric acid cycle and the respiratory chain down, glycolysis and lactate up. The oxidase knockout rescues it, so it is a ROS-driven arm and not a correlation.</data><data key="d5">PMID 40286622</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="520.0" y="75.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="70.0" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="228.0" x="6.0" xml:space="preserve" y="10.0" hasText="true">The heart's fuel switch:
beta-oxidation and the
respiratory chain down,
glycolysis up<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_gr-signalling"><data key="d0">gr-signalling</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">8</data><data key="d3">general physiology only</data><data key="d4">cortisol is an output of two entry modules and acts on nothing in the map. No noise study measured glucocorticoid receptor target genes, so every arc would be physiology: the smooth muscle receptor is needed for glucocorticoid hypertension, and 11-beta-HSD2 loss is pro-inflammatory in the endothelium.</data><data key="d5">PMID 18434569, PMID 21106873</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="1040.0" y="225.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="53.5" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="256.5" x="-8.25" xml:space="preserve" y="18.25" hasText="true">What cortisol does: the
glucocorticoid receptor and
the NF-kB brake<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_kidney-glomerulus"><data key="d0">kidney-glomerulus</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">9</data><data key="d3">measured, but under low-frequency noise</data><data key="d4">a second organ for the endothelin output, with its own measured injury: raised creatinine and urea, higher renal EDN1 and NOS3, a thickened basement membrane and mesangial expansion, all mitigated by endothelin inhibition. The exposure was the low-frequency component of environmental noise, not transportation noise, which is why it ranks here.</data><data key="d5">PMID 42312733, PMID 33623977</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="520.0" y="525.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="53.5" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="256.5" x="-8.25" xml:space="preserve" y="18.25" hasText="true">Glomerular injury through
endothelin: the endothelial
and mesangial cell<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_myeloid-nox2"><data key="d0">myeloid-nox2</data><data key="d1">planned</data><data key="d2">3</data><data key="d3">measured under noise, with perturbation</data><data key="d4">the map puts NOX2 in the endothelium and in microglia, but the field's central result is that the circulating myeloid oxidase is the one that matters; and the heart module needs a monocyte that arrives with its own oxidase. Ablating lysozyme M-positive cells prevents the vascular damage, and the oxidase knockout normalises leucocyte adhesion in vivo. The marrow step above it (noradrenaline, beta-3 receptor, CXCL12 down, monocyte output) is from chronic variable stress, not from noise.</data><data key="d5">PMID 33929610, PMID 34274810, PMID 29905797, PMID 24952646</data><data key="ng">
            <y:GenericNode configuration="com.yworks.sbgn.Submap">
              <y:Geometry height="90.0" width="240.0" x="1560.0" y="75.0"/>
              <y:Fill color="#FFFFFF" transparent="false"/>
              <y:BorderStyle color="#000000" type="line" width="1.0"/>
              <y:NodeLabel alignment="center" autoSizePolicy="content" fontFamily="Dialog" fontSize="14" fontStyle="plain" hasBackgroundColor="false" hasLineColor="false" height="70.0" horizontalTextPosition="center" iconTextGap="4" modelName="custom" textColor="#000000" verticalTextPosition="center" visible="true" width="256.5" x="-8.25" xml:space="preserve" y="10.0" hasText="true">The myelomonocytic oxidase:
the cell that brings NOX2
into the wall, and how it
adheres<y:LabelModel><y:SmartNodeLabelModel distance="4.0"/></y:LabelModel><y:ModelParameter><y:SmartNodeLabelModelParameter labelRatioX="0.0" labelRatioY="0.0" nodeRatioX="0.0" nodeRatioY="0.0" offsetX="0.0" offsetY="0.0" upX="0.0" upY="-1.0"/></y:ModelParameter></y:NodeLabel>
            </y:GenericNode>
          </data>
        </node><node id="m_noise-brain"><data key="d0">noise-brain</data><data key="d1">noise-brain-v01.graphml</data><data key="ng">
